{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target begs the question it was asked to address. The claim asserts greater resilience to correlated failure specifically because shared fate is eliminated, yet the mechanism merely restates that premise ('failure surface is fragmented... boundaries of independent administrative domains'). This provides no argument or evidence distinguishing truly independent failure modes from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found — the exact evidentiary burden the problem sets. Worse, the target's own counterconditions (shared third-party DNS/cloud region, common zero-day in a standardized stack) concede that the 'shared fate' the claim says is 'eliminated' can in fact persist across decentralized services, undercutting the causal claim and leaving no basis for preferring decentralization over strict internal redundancy under correlated causes.","target":"38258a4fae2f1bd22e727f050933ce41e6a18e5e709680dbc4ab4850ca045598"}]}